WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:03.750
Let us now see how tests
can also be applied

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:07.710
to categorical variables,
rather than quantitative measures

3
00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:11.990
such as means, using the chi-square
test of independence.

4
00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:17.250
For example, we can ask
whether the presence of a

5
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:22.780
species is associated
with a particular type of habitat.

6
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.420
We have three types of habitat:
forest, grassland,

7
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.560
and cropland. We have 40
observations in each habitat,

8
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.500
recording whether or not
the species is present.

9
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.820
In our forest sample,
out of 40 observations,

10
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.560
the species was present 30
times and absent 10 times.

11
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.000
For grassland, the counts are 20
and 20; for cropland, 10 and 30.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.680
Those are our observations.

13
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.980
Our null hypothesis, H0, is

14
00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:00.580
that the presence of the species
is independent of its habitat.

15
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.900
To test this hypothesis, we compare

16
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.340
the matrix we obtained here

17
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:14.360
with the matrix of expected
counts under H0,

18
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:20.740
where the proportion of the species
is the same in every habitat type.

19
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.740
We perform the calculation and compare

20
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.740
these two matrices
to obtain the test statistic

21
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.780
and decide whether to reject
the chi-square test's null hypothesis.

22
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.040
We can display this graphically,

23
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.080
showing our 40 observations
and the proportion

24
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:50.000
in which the species
is present in each habitat.

25
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.780
Here we can see
the chi-square calculation,

26
00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.500
comparing observed and expected counts.

27
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:04.020
Again, the differences
are squared so that positive

28
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:09.240
and negative values do not cancel out,
then divided by the expected value,

29
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:14.460
accounting for degrees of freedom
from the observations and groups analysed.

30
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.280
This gives us a certain

31
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.160
chi statistic with a very
small associated p-value,

32
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.900
much smaller than
our chosen threshold of 55%.

33
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.740
We therefore reject H0 that the presence

34
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.540
of our species is independent
of its habitat.
